From 6f4dc48281034045ca817dc06905af78ac215da6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch <patch@local>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 23:02:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Split browse filters into two rows: who took it, what's on it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The chip row had to answer two different questions with one selection,
so picking Abby meant giving up the favorites filter and vice versa. A
new reaction makes that worse: "Abby's photos somebody laughed at" is a
question the gallery couldn't ask at all.

Favorites moves out of the people row into a second row alongside one
chip per reaction. The rows combine with AND -- Abby + 😂 is Abby's
photos that at least one person laughed at.

Semantics worth being explicit about, because the two marks differ:

  - A reaction chip matches when *anyone* reacted. The DTO's tally
    already counts every user's reaction, and "someone laughed at this"
    is the interesting fact; "I laughed at this" is a much smaller and
    less useful set. `myReaction` is untouched and still drives the
    corner badge.
  - A star chip can only mean yours. Favorites are private by
    construction, so there's no other reading available.

Within a row the choice stays exclusive. 😂 + ❤️ would have to mean
either "both" or "either", and a lit chip has no way to say which -- so
each row is a radio group, and tapping the lit chip clears it.

Counts are now faceted: every chip is measured against the *other*
row's filter, so its number answers "how many photos would tapping this
leave?". Select 😂 and Abby's chip drops from 284 to her laughed-at
photos. Counting each row against itself would light a chip reading 284
and then show you nine. Chips never disappear at zero -- a row that
reshuffles as you tap it is hard to aim at -- and the avatar's existing
grey-at-zero rendering does the work of showing a dead end.

The reaction chips are generated from REACTIONS in ReactionBar rather
than a list spelled out here, so an emoji added to the bar gets its
filter chip, its tally, and its glyph with no further edits. Browse and
PhotoPanel each carried a private copy of that emoji table for the
corner badge; both now look it up, which is the bug this feature would
otherwise have hit first -- a new reaction renders as an empty span
until someone remembers the other two files.

The empty state changes with it. With two filters combining, an empty
gallery is usually a combination rather than a shortage, so it offers
"Clear filters" instead of inviting an upload -- except when there
genuinely are no photos, or when the only filter is a star nobody has
placed yet.
